From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AndrewsN81 <nathan.andrews81@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 21:08:17 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] metal: compile shader libraries off the render thread
 (opt-in); drawables wait instead of blocking

Every Metal program is compiled from source on first use with a synchronous
newLibrary, on the render thread - which on iOS is the main thread. With a
cold shader cache that is ~0.4-0.6 s per program, sequentially: a first
launch stalled the UI for seconds, and a 3D-terrain style's first draped
frame (terrain + depth pass + new symbol permutations) for over a second.

gfx::Context::setAsyncShaderCompilation(bool) (Metal implements it) hands
back a ShaderProgram whose functions arrive from Metal's asynchronous
newLibrary completion handler (ShaderProgram::PendingFunctions, adopted on
the render thread). Drawables whose program is not ready skip the frame,
and Renderer keeps requesting frames while Context::hasPendingShaderCompiles.
The clipping mask program stays synchronous (drawn outside the drawable
path). Off by default so single-frame renders (render tests, snapshots)
are unchanged; MLNMapView turns it on via Renderer::setAsyncShaderCompilation.
Compile durations are logged at Info.

Measured on the iOS simulator with the shader cache cleared: 13 programs
compiled concurrently off-thread at 400-560 ms each, map identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
